A.1.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
A.1.1. {"problem": "The change removes `requires: human` from `decide`, so an agent can now settle a decision request. Two descriptions still say only a person may take it. `DecisionRequest` is described as 'A decision only a person may take, proposed with grounds and a recommendation and sent to that person's outbox', and `request_decision` as 'Ask the owner for a decision only a person may take'. Both now contradict `decide`.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: objects.DecisionRequest.description; actions.request_decision.description; actions.decide", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.1.2. {"problem": "The rule that an agent may settle only when the owner said so in chat, quoting the owner's words, is not enforced. `owner_words` is optional and has no precondition, so any caller can decide without quoting anything. The only actor is still `owner`, so nothing separates an agent from the owner, and the quote is not stored on the request. `records_decision: true` remains, so the record would show the owner decided even when an agent did. The human-only guarantee was dropped without a replacement check.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide (params.owner_words, missing precondition, actor)", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.1.3. {"problem": "Removing `requires: human` makes `decide` the only decision-taking action that no longer requires a person. `confirm_element`, `approve_commit`, `skip_stage` and `confirm_all_proposed` still require one. A decision request is tied to an element, and `confirm_element` carries a second-person rule for approve/deploy elements. An agent could now settle a request about such an element on one person's chat word, which weakens that approach.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide vs actions.confirm_element (permission-needs-second-person) and the other `requires: human` actions", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.1.4. {"problem": "The diff sets the changed `decide` to `status: proposed` with a new interview source (L58), but the after-change file shows `status: confirmed`. The two disagree. If it is really confirmed, the weakening of a human-only rule is marked confirmed by an interview line I cannot verify, and the old record-based source for 'only a person decides' has been replaced. If it is proposed, the transition cells that depend on `decide` (open->decided, decided->deny) stay confirmed on a proposed action.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide status/source; transitions.DecisionRequest", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.1.5. {"problem": "The interface changed: `decide` gained a param and lost its human-only requirement. The domain `version` stayed at 23, and the composition pins `interface: 1`. The change is not versioned, so deployed consumers cannot see it.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: version; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.1.6. {"problem": "No scenario covers the new path. The only decide scenario, `a-decision-is-taken-once`, calls `decide` without `owner_words`. Nothing tests an agent settling with the owner's words, or a refusal without them.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: scenarios.a-decision-is-taken-once", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.2.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
A.2.1. {"problem": "The change drops `requires: human` from `decide`, but the domain still says this is a decision only a person may take. The DecisionRequest description and the `request_decision` description both say so. An agent can now settle it, which contradicts both descriptions. Nothing was updated to reflect the new rule.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide vs objects.DecisionRequest.description and actions.request_decision.description", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.2.2. {"problem": "The rule that an agent acts only when the owner said so in chat is not enforced. `owner_words` is optional and there is no precondition requiring it to exist and be non-empty. The actor is still just `owner`, so anyone, agent or not, can call `decide` with no quote and settle the request. Removing `requires: human` therefore opens the action completely. Compare `confirm_wording_fix`, which guards its agent path with a precondition, and `skip_stage`, which requires a reason.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide (params.owner_words, missing preconditions)", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.2.3. {"problem": "A decision request can be about an element that governs who may approve or deploy (approve_commit, deliver, deploy). `confirm_element` requires a human and a second person for those elements. An agent can now settle the linked decision request without any human, which gets around that two-person rule. The change does not restrict this case.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide vs actions.confirm_element (permission-needs-second-person) and links.element", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.2.4. {"problem": "The `decide` entry is marked `status: confirmed` in the file after the change, but the diff sets it to `proposed`, so the file and the diff disagree. The entry also cites a new interview line (L58) in place of the ADR 0032 record that justified the human-only rule. If it really is confirmed, a safety rule was loosened and marked confirmed with no person confirming it. It should be `proposed` until the owner confirms it. The L58 citation should also be checked, since this change request gives no reason for it.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide source/status", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.2.5. {"problem": "`decide` still has `records_decision: true`, but the record will not show whether the owner or an agent decided. `owner_words` is only a param and is not stored on DecisionRequest or in the decision record. The audit trail cannot show who actually settled the request or on what words.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide effects / objects.DecisionRequest", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.2.6. {"problem": "No scenario covers the new agent path: with owner_words, without owner_words, or by a non-owner. The glossary and other text that says only a person decides are not updated. The domain `version` stays at 23 although the action's parameters and permissions changed.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: scenarios, glossary, version", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.3.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
A.3.1. {"problem": "The change removes `requires: human` from `decide`, so an agent can now settle a decision request. Two descriptions still say only a person may take the decision: DecisionRequest (\"A decision only a person may take\") and request_decision (\"Ask the owner for a decision only a person may take\"). Both now contradict `decide`.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: objects.DecisionRequest.description and actions.request_decision.description", "kind": "reference", "from": "llm"}
A.3.2. {"problem": "The description says an agent may settle only when the owner has said so in chat, quoting the owner's words in `owner_words`. But `owner_words` is optional and no precondition requires it. With `requires: human` gone, any caller acting as owner can decide without quoting anything. The condition is not enforced, and nothing separates an agent's decision from the owner's own. `records_decision: true` also no longer shows that a person decided. `confirm_wording_fix` guards its agent path with a precondition; `decide` has none.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide (params.owner_words, preconditions, requires)", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.3.3. {"problem": "The `decide` action's status disagrees with the diff. The diff sets it to `status: proposed`, but the file after the change shows `status: confirmed`. The new source is an interview line (L58) with no quote. A rule that loosens a human-only guard should not appear confirmed unless a person has confirmed it.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide.status and source", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.3.4. {"problem": "The change loosens a human-only decision and alters the interface of `decide` by adding a param, but the domain `version` stays at 23. Delivery and the composition (`interface: 1`) are not touched either. The change is not marked as a new version.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: version; toprope-self.composition.yaml: members.authoring.interface", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
