A.4.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
A.4.1. {"problem": "The change drops `requires: human` from `decide`, but the DecisionRequest object still says it is 'A decision only a person may take', and `request_decision` says 'Ask the owner for a decision only a person may take'. The domain now says two opposite things about who may decide. The `decide` rule no longer matches the 'only a person' wording, and `confirm_element` and `confirm_all_proposed` keep `requires: human`.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: objects.DecisionRequest description, actions.request_decision description, actions.decide (requires removed)", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.4.2. {"problem": "The description says an agent may settle a decision when the owner said so in chat and `owner_words` quotes it. Nothing enforces this. `owner_words` is optional and there is no precondition requiring it, so a caller can decide with no quote. The `actor` list is still only `owner`, so an agent has no role through which to act on the owner's behalf, unlike `confirm_wording_fix`, which has a precondition limiting what an agent may do. The `owner_words` text is also not recorded anywhere (no effect or property stores it), so the decision record cannot show the owner authorised it.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide params.owner_words, preconditions, effects, actor", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.4.3. {"problem": "Removing the human requirement opens a bypass of the two-person rule. `confirm_element` requires a human and a second confirmer for elements about approve_commit, deliver or deploy. DecisionRequests link to Elements, and `decide` sets only a choice. An agent can now settle a decision about such an element on the strength of a chat remark, without a person acting and without a second person. `decide` also has `records_decision: true`, so the decision would be recorded against an agent.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide vs actions.confirm_element (permission-needs-second-person), link element", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.4.4. {"problem": "The diff sets `status: proposed` and a new interview source (L58) with no quote. The file as it stands shows `status: confirmed` for `decide`. The weakening of a human-only rule is therefore marked confirmed with no recorded person's confirmation, or the diff and the file disagree. The old confirmed record source (ADR-0032) was also replaced rather than kept.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: actions.decide source/status", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.4.5. {"problem": "A new optional param was added to `decide` and its behaviour changed, but the authoring domain `version` stays 23. The glossary and the scenario `a-decision-is-taken-once` were not updated. No scenario covers an agent deciding with `owner_words`, or without it, so the new path is untested.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: version, scenarios, glossary", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.5.0. {"domain": "authoring", "check": "structural", "severity": "warning", "where": "actions.add_source.params", "problem": "unset-on-create: add_source creates a Source without its required `local_only` (an optional param sets it): make the param required, set it in an effect, or make the property optional", "carried": true, "from": "compiler"}
A.5.1. {"problem": "The change drops `requires: human` from `decide`, so any caller in the owner role can now settle a decision, and nothing enforces the new rule. `owner_words` is optional and no precondition requires it, so an agent can decide with no quoted owner words and no sign the owner said so in chat. The \"agent only with the owner's words\" rule exists only in the description.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action decide (params.owner_words, missing precondition)", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
A.5.2. {"problem": "Other text still says a decision is one only a person takes. `DecisionRequest` is described as \"A decision only a person may take\", and `request_decision` says \"Ask the owner for a decision only a person may take\". Both now contradict `decide`, which lets an agent settle it.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, object DecisionRequest description; action request_decision description", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.5.3. {"problem": "The `decide` action no longer needs a human, but a decision request is linked to an element, and elements about who may approve or deploy (approve_commit, deliver, deploy) need two people to confirm via `confirm_element`. An agent can now settle the decision behind such an element alone, which sidesteps the human-only and second-person rules in `confirm_element` (`requires: human`, `permission-needs-second-person`).", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action decide vs confirm_element / permission-needs-second-person; link DecisionRequest.element", "kind": "contradiction", "from": "llm"}
A.5.4. {"problem": "The diff sets `decide` to `status: proposed` with an unquoted interview source, but the file after the change still shows `status: confirmed`. The two disagree, so the weakened rule may be deployed as confirmed without anyone confirming it. The transitions for `decide` and the scenario `a-decision-is-taken-once` still cite confirmed sources and have not been revisited.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action decide status/source", "kind": "other", "from": "llm"}
A.5.5. {"problem": "The record of who decided is unclear. `decide` still has `records_decision: true`, but when an agent acts the actor recorded is the agent, not the owner. The agent is also not required to say which owner approved, since `owner_words` is optional and unattributed. The record can therefore show a decision as the owner's when it was not.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action decide (records_decision, actor)", "kind": "behaviour", "from": "llm"}
