B.1.0. {"problem": "The new agent-only action `decide_from_chat` settles a DecisionRequest without `requires: human`. That contradicts the rule that these decisions belong to a person: DecisionRequest is described as 'A decision only a person may take', and request_decision as 'a decision only a person may take'. `decide` stays human-only, yet an agent can now do the same thing by quoting any non-empty string. The 'Human-only actions' list and the 'Actions that record a decision' list now disagree about who may record a decision. The only guard is `owner_words != ''`, which cannot show the owner actually said it, so the agent is in effect choosing for the owner.", "where": "SKILL.md, decide_from_chat; DecisionRequest object; request_decision; 'Human-only actions' line", "kind": "contradiction"}
B.1.1. {"problem": "The owner's words are required but never stored. The effect sets only target.choice, and DecisionRequest has no property for the quote. Nothing records afterwards what the owner said or that the decision came from chat rather than the terminal, so a decision recorded by an agent cannot be audited or told apart from one the owner took.", "where": "SKILL.md, decide_from_chat effect and DecisionRequest properties", "kind": "behaviour"}
B.1.2. {"problem": "The example only covers the empty-quote denial, the successful case and the second decision. It never shows that an agent is refused when there is no real owner statement. It also does not check that `decide` is still refused to the agent, so the example implies the whole path is safe when the rule above is not enforced.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, an-agent-settles-a-decision-the-owner-gave-in-chat", "kind": "other"}
B.2.0. {"problem": "The new action lets an agent record a decision on a DecisionRequest, which the skill defines as a decision only a person may take. `decide` is `requires: human` and is listed under Human-only actions. `decide_from_chat` is not `requires: human` and is not in that list. The only gate is that `owner_words` is non-empty, which cannot be checked, so an agent can write any text and settle the request itself. It is also listed under 'Actions that record a decision' while being absent from the human-only list.", "where": "SKILL.md, decide_from_chat, 'Human-only actions' line, and the DecisionRequest object and request_decision description", "kind": "contradiction"}
B.2.1. {"problem": "The quoted owner words are not stored anywhere. The effect only sets target.choice, and DecisionRequest has no property for the owner's words or for who settled it. After the action, nothing on record shows that the choice came from the owner rather than the agent, so the 'quote the owner' rule cannot be audited. The `decided-requests-record-the-choice` invariant is satisfied either way.", "where": "SKILL.md, decide_from_chat effect and DecisionRequest properties", "kind": "other"}
B.2.2. {"problem": "The precondition `owner-words-written` only checks that the string is non-empty. The example treats `\"Actually B\"` as a valid quote. Nothing ties the words to the choice or to the right request, so the example does not show the rule it claims to show.", "where": "SKILL.md decide_from_chat precondition `owner-words-written`; EXAMPLES.md an-agent-settles-a-decision-the-owner-gave-in-chat", "kind": "behaviour"}
B.3.0. {"problem": "decide is human-only (requires: human) because a decision must be taken by a person. decide_from_chat is a new agent action that records a decision and sets the same choice, but it is not human-only. The agent supplies only a free-text quote, which nothing verifies, so any agent can settle a request by writing any non-empty string. This bypasses the human-only guarantee on decisions. The 'Human-only actions' list was not updated and no rule explains the exception.", "where": "SKILL.md, decide_from_chat and the 'Human-only actions' / 'Actions that record a decision' lines", "kind": "contradiction"}
B.3.1. {"problem": "The new action's reason and description say the owner is not asked again on the terminal, yet decide remains the only human-confirmed path. The decided-requests-record-the-choice invariant and the 'already decided' denials treat both actions the same, so a chat decision cannot be told apart from a person's decision. The owner's words are not stored on the DecisionRequest (no property for them), so the quote required by owner-words-written is discarded and leaves no audit trail.", "where": "SKILL.md, decide_from_chat Effect (only sets choice) / DecisionRequest object", "kind": "other"}
B.3.2. {"problem": "The example's step 1 passes owner_words as an empty string, but the precondition uses exists(params.owner_words) and != ''. That works, but the example never covers the case where the actor is not an agent, and there is no check that choice is non-empty.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md, an-agent-settles-a-decision-the-owner-gave-in-chat; SKILL.md decide_from_chat preconditions", "kind": "other"}
B.3.3. {"problem": "The glossary and the human-only-decision wording elsewhere (e.g. approve_commit 'Only a person approves') are unchanged, but the DecisionRequest description still says 'A decision only a person may take'. The new action lets an agent take it, which contradicts that object definition.", "where": "SKILL.md, DecisionRequest object description", "kind": "contradiction"}
B.4.0. {"problem": "decide is human-only (`requires: human`) so a person's own decision is the only way a decision is recorded. The new decide_from_chat lets an agent record a decision on a DecisionRequest ('a decision only a person may take') with no `requires: human`. The only guard is that owner_words is non-empty, so any text suffices and nothing verifies the owner said it. It is also listed under 'Actions that record a decision' but not under 'Human-only actions', which now contradicts the DecisionRequest definition and the human-only decision rule.", "where": "SKILL.md, decide_from_chat; DecisionRequest object description; 'Human-only actions' and 'Actions that record a decision' lines", "kind": "contradiction"}
B.4.1. {"problem": "The owner's words are required as a parameter but are never stored. DecisionRequest has no property for them and the Effect sets only choice, so the quote is lost and there is no audit trail. The invariant 'decided-requests-record-the-choice' is met, but nothing records who or what justified the decision.", "where": "SKILL.md, decide_from_chat Effect; DecisionRequest properties", "kind": "other"}
